                '<p class="text-lg text-gray-600">Choosing the operating system for your web hosting infrastructure '
                'dictates server stability, tenant security, and software maintenance overhead. While AlmaLinux has '
                "emerged as the premier free, open-source enterprise Linux distribution following CentOS's "
                'discontinuation, CloudLinux OS remains the commercial standard for multi-tenant shared hosting. This '
                'guide examines how they compare in architecture, multi-tenancy, and operational value.</p>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">CloudLinux vs AlmaLinux: Core '
                'Architecture Differences</h2>\n'
                '  <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200"><table class="w-full min-w-[34rem] '
                'text-sm"><thead><tr><th class="border-b border-gray-200 bg-mist px-4 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold '
                'uppercase tracking-wider text-gray-600">Feature</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 bg-mist px-4 '
                'py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-gray-600">AlmaLinux (Open '
                'Source)</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 bg-mist px-4 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold '
                'uppercase tracking-wider text-gray-600">CloudLinux OS (Commercial)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td '
                'class="border-b border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">License Cost</td><td class="border-b '
                'border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">100% Free &amp; Open Source</td><td class="border-b '
                'border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">Paid commercial per-server license</td></tr><tr><td '
                'class="border-b border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">Multi-Tenant Isolation</td><td class="border-b '
                'border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">Standard Linux user permissions</td><td class="border-b '
                'border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">CageFS virtualized file system per user</td></tr><tr><td '
                'class="border-b border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">Resource Limits (LVE)</td><td class="border-b '
                'border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">No native per-user CPU/RAM capping</td><td class="border-b '
                'border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">Granular LVE CPU, RAM, IO &amp; IOPS limits</td></tr><tr><td '
                'class="border-b border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">Database Protection</td><td class="border-b '
                'border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">Standard MySQL / MariaDB</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 '
                'px-4 py-3 align-top">MySQL Governor real-time query throttling</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b '
                'border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">PHP Management</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 '
                'align-top">Single system PHP or basic multi-PHP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 '
                'align-top">PHP Selector with hardened legacy version patches</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b '
                'border-gray-100 px-4 py-3 align-top">Target Use Case</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 px-4 '
                'py-3 align-top">Single-tenant VPS, internal servers, apps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 '
                'px-4 py-3 align-top">Commercial shared &amp; reseller web hosting</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n'
                '  <figure class="my-2"><img src="/assets/img/blog/cloudlinux-vs-almalinux-1.svg" alt="Visual '
                'comparison of CloudLinux LVE CageFS containerization versus standard shared Linux" width="960" '
                'height="420" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-gray-200" '
                '/><figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-xs text-gray-500">Multi-tenant resource isolation '
                'compared.</figcaption></figure>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">The "Noisy Neighbor" Problem '
                'and LVE Limits</h2>\n'
                '  <p>On a standard Linux operating system like AlmaLinux, all hosting accounts share the same global '
                'pool of server memory and CPU threads. If one client experiences a sudden traffic spike, runs an '
                'unoptimized database query, or suffers a brute-force attack on WordPress, their processes can consume '
                '100% of server resources, crashing Apache and taking down all other websites on the server.</p>\n'
                '  <p>CloudLinux solves this with <strong>Lightweight Virtual Environments (LVE)</strong>. LVE places '
                'hard kernel-level ceilings on CPU cores, RAM, entry processes, and disk I/O for each account. If '
                'Tenant A maxes out their allocated 1 Core and 1GB RAM, only Tenant A receives temporary 503 '
                'throttles; all other websites on the server remain blazingly fast and responsive.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">CageFS: Total File System '
                'Security Isolation</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Under standard Linux user permissions, tenants can navigate system directories, inspect running '
                'process lists with <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">ps aux</code>, '
                'and view server configuration files containing database connection strings. In shared hosting, a '
                'single compromised website can expose sensitive files across the entire partition.</p>\n'
                "  <p>CloudLinux's <strong>CageFS</strong> encapsulates each user inside an isolated sandbox. Tenants "
                'can only view their own files and safe system binaries. They cannot see other users, view root '
                'directories, or snoop on server environment variables, neutralizing privilege escalation and '
                'cross-account contamination.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">PHP Selector and Hardened PHP '
                'Versions</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Hosting clients often run legacy web applications requiring older PHP versions (such as PHP 7.4 '
                'or 7.2) alongside modern sites running PHP 8.2 or 8.3. While upstream PHP officially deprecates older '
                "branches, CloudLinux's <strong>Hardened PHP</strong> backports critical security fixes to legacy "
                'versions.</p>\n'
                '  <p>With CloudLinux\'s <strong>PHP Selector</strong> inside <a href="/cpanel-license" '
                'class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">cPanel</a>, end users can select their desired PHP '
                'version and toggle individual PHP extensions directly from their control panel without needing root '
                'administrator intervention.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">When Should You Use AlmaLinux '
                'vs CloudLinux?</h2>\n'
                '  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">\n'
                '    <li><strong>Deploy AlmaLinux</strong> when you run single-tenant application servers, private VPS '
                'nodes, internal corporate portals, or dedicated servers hosting trusted internal websites.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>Deploy CloudLinux</strong> whenever you host untrusted third-party customers, offer '
                'shared hosting plans, run reseller accounts, or want to dramatically reduce server crashes caused by '
                'misbehaving scripts.</li>\n'
                '  </ul>\n'
                '  <p>Because converting from AlmaLinux to CloudLinux takes only minutes using the official <code '
                'class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">cldeploy</code> script, you can deploy '
                'your server on AlmaLinux today and upgrade with an affordable <a href="/cloudlinux-license" '
                'class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">CloudLinux license</a> from LicenBase when your '
                'customer base expands. Pair your setup with <a href="/litespeed-license" class="font-semibold '
                'text-brand hover:underline">LiteSpeed</a> for complete server acceleration.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n',
