                '<p class="text-lg text-gray-600">Setting up your first Linux Virtual Private Server is an exciting '
                'milestone, but an unhardened server is an easy target for automated botnets, cryptocurrency miners, '
                'and malicious scanners. Protecting your server does not require decades of cybersecurity expertise. '
                'Following this beginner-friendly 20-point checklist establishes a rock-solid security foundation that '
                'safeguards your websites, client data, and server uptime.</p>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">20 Essential VPS Security '
                'Foundations</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Securing a Linux server begins with identity access control, network firewalling, routine '
                'updates, and automated application-level malware defense.</p>\n'
                '  <figure class="my-2"><img src="/assets/img/blog/secure-new-vps-20-security-tips-beginners-1.svg" '
                'alt="20 foundational security tips and hardening checklist for a new Linux VPS server" width="960" '
                'height="420" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-gray-200" '
                '/><figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-xs text-gray-500">Comprehensive 20-point security '
                'checklist for beginners configuring a Linux VPS.</figcaption></figure>\n'
                '  <p>Implementing these 20 foundational steps ensures that your infrastructure is protected against '
                '99% of automated internet threats.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">20 Beginner-Friendly VPS '
                'Security Tips</h2>\n'
                '  <ol class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">\n'
                '    <li><strong>1. Update All System Packages:</strong> Run <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 '
                'py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">dnf update -y</code> or <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 '
                'text-[0.85em] text-navy">apt update &amp;&amp; apt upgrade -y</code> immediately after '
                'provisioning.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>2. Create a Dedicated Sudo User:</strong> Create an unprivileged user and avoid '
                'logging in as root directly.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>3. Enforce ED25519 SSH Keys:</strong> Replace passwords with cryptographic SSH key '
                'pairs.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>4. Disable Root Password Login:</strong> Set <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 '
                'py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">PermitRootLogin no</code> in <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 '
                'py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code>.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>5. Relocate the Default SSH Port:</strong> Move SSH from port 22 to a non-standard '
                'port (such as 2244) to stop 95% of automated bot scans.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>6. Deploy a Software Firewall:</strong> Install UFW or CSF to block all unauthorized '
                'ports by default.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>7. Enable Fail2ban:</strong> Automatically ban IP addresses that repeatedly enter '
                'wrong passwords.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>8. Enable Automated Security Updates:</strong> Use <code class="rounded bg-mist '
                'px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">dnf-automatic</code> or <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 '
                'py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">unattended-upgrades</code> for hands-free patching.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>9. Secure Shared Memory (/dev/shm):</strong> Mount shared memory with <code '
                'class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">noexec,nosuid</code> to prevent '
                'execution of unauthorized payloads.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>10. Disable Unneeded Services:</strong> Turn off unused services like Telnet, FTP, or '
                'unused mail daemons.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>11. Install an Anti-Malware WAF Suite:</strong> Deploy an <a '
                'href="/imunify360-license" class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">Imunify360 license</a> '
                'for real-time automated malware scanning and proactive PHP defense.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>12. Isolate Multi-Tenant Accounts:</strong> Use a <a href="/cloudlinux-license" '
                'class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">CloudLinux OS license</a> with CageFS to encapsulate '
                'each website into a virtual sandbox.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>13. Disable Dangerous PHP Functions:</strong> Turn off <code class="rounded bg-mist '
                'px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">exec, shell_exec, system</code> in your global PHP '
                'configuration.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>14. Enforce Strong Passwords:</strong> Mandate minimum 16-character complex passwords '
                'for databases and control panels.</li>\n'
                "    <li><strong>15. Install SSL Certificates for All Domains:</strong> Use automated Let's Encrypt / "
                'AutoSSL for HTTPS encryption.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>16. Set Up Automated Offsite Backups:</strong> Schedule daily backups using <a '
                'href="/jetbackup-license" class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">JetBackup</a> to remote '
                'cloud storage.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>17. Enable 2-Factor Authentication (2FA):</strong> Require 2FA on your control panel '
                '(<a href="/cpanel-license" class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">cPanel &amp; WHM</a>) and '
                'billing accounts.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>18. Protect WordPress Login Endpoints:</strong> Restrict <code class="rounded bg-mist '
                'px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">wp-login.php</code> and disable <code class="rounded bg-mist '
                'px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">xmlrpc.php</code>.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>19. Monitor Listening Network Ports:</strong> Periodically run <code class="rounded '
                'bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">ss -tulpn</code> to verify only authorized ports are '
                'open.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>20. Keep Clean Architecture Backups:</strong> Keep offsite snapshots of server '
                'configuration files to enable rapid disaster recovery.</li>\n'
                '  </ol>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">Summary Defense '
                'Checklist</h2>\n'
                '  <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200"><table class="w-full min-w-[34rem] '
                'text-sm"><thead><tr><th class="border-b border-gray-200 bg-mist px-4 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold '
                'uppercase tracking-wider text-gray-600">Security Pillar</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 '
