                '<p class="text-lg text-gray-600">Unexplained server crashes, random reboots, and sudden unresponsive '
                'freezes are among the most stressful issues a system administrator can face. When a production Linux '
                'VPS goes offline unexpectedly, website uptime suffers, transactions are interrupted, and database '
                'tables risk corruption. Investigating a crash requires forensic analysis of system logs from the '
                'moments leading up to the failure. This guide explores the 12 most frequent root causes of VPS '
                'crashes and provides practical fixes to ensure rock-solid stability.</p>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">1. Forensic Analysis: '
                'Inspecting Previous Boot Logs</h2>\n'
                '  <p>When your VPS reboots or recovers from a freeze, immediately inspect systemd journal logs from '
                'the previous boot session:</p>\n'
                '  <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-navy p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-100"><code># '
                'View logs from the previous boot session leading up to the crash\n'
                'journalctl -b -1 -e\n'
                '\n'
                '# Search for kernel panic messages and hardware errors\n'
                'journalctl -b -1 -k | grep -E -i "panic|oom|error|killed"\n'
                '\n'
                '# Inspect system messages log on RHEL/AlmaLinux\n'
                'grep -i "killed process" /var/log/messages</code></pre>\n'
                '  <p>Pay close attention to the last entries recorded before the timestamp reset. If the logs end '
                'abruptly without any shutdown signals, the VPS hypervisor forcefully rebooted the instance due to '
                'hardware or host-node resource limits.</p>\n'
                '  <figure class="my-2"><img src="/assets/img/blog/vps-server-keeps-crashing-causes-fixes-1.svg" '
                'alt="Linux VPS crash troubleshooting flowchart" width="960" height="420" loading="lazy" '
                'decoding="async" class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-gray-200" /><figcaption class="mt-2 '
                'text-center text-xs text-gray-500">Troubleshooting OOM killer events, kernel panics, hypervisor '
                'steal, and I/O wait timeouts.</figcaption></figure>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">2. The Linux Out-of-Memory '
                '(OOM) Killer</h2>\n'
                '  <p>The single most common cause of sudden service crashes (particularly MySQL/MariaDB and Apache) '
                'is the Linux OOM Killer. When physical RAM is exhausted and swap is disabled or full, the kernel '
                'invokes <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">out_of_memory()</code> to '
                'sacrifice memory-heavy processes.</p>\n'
                '  <p>Diagnose OOM events with these commands:</p>\n'
                '  <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-navy p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-100"><code># '
                'Check dmesg for OOM killer execution\n'
                'dmesg -T | grep -i "out of memory"\n'
                '\n'
                '# Verify current memory and swap allocation\n'
                'free -h</code></pre>\n'
                '  <p>To prevent OOM crashes:</p>\n'
                '  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">\n'
                '    <li><strong>Configure Swap Space:</strong> Add at least 2GB to 4GB of swap space on SSD/NVMe '
                'storage to give the kernel headroom during traffic bursts.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>Tune MySQL InnoDB Buffer:</strong> Lower <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 '
                'text-[0.85em] text-navy">innodb_buffer_pool_size</code> in <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 '
                'text-[0.85em] text-navy">my.cnf</code> so database memory does not exceed available RAM.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>Adjust OOM Score for Critical Daemons:</strong> Set <code class="rounded bg-mist '
                'px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">OOMScoreAdjust=-1000</code> in <code class="rounded bg-mist '
                'px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">sshd.service</code> and <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 '
                'py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">mysqld.service</code> unit files so the kernel prefers killing '
                'transient PHP workers instead of database daemons.</li>\n'
                '  </ul>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">3. Storage I/O Bottlenecks and '
                'Uninterruptible Sleep (D-State)</h2>\n'
                '  <p>When storage throughput is saturated by database table locks or unthrottled backup creation, '
                'processes enter an uninterruptible sleep state (represented as <strong>D</strong> in <code '
                'class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">ps</code>). As processes queue waiting '
                'for disk acknowledgments, system load average climbs to 50+, making SSH unresponsive:</p>\n'
                '  <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-navy p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-100"><code># '
                'Monitor real-time disk I/O wait and queue depth\n'
                'iostat -xz 1 5\n'
                '\n'
                '# List all processes currently locked in D-state\n'
                'ps r -A</code></pre>\n'
                '  <p>If %util reaches 100% or await exceeds 20ms consistently, optimize database queries, switch to '
                'an asynchronous web server like <a href="/litespeed-license" class="font-semibold text-brand '
                'hover:underline">LiteSpeed</a>, or migrate your VPS to high-speed Enterprise NVMe storage.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">4. Hypervisor "Noisy Neighbor" '
                'and High CPU Steal</h2>\n'
                '  <p>On budget VPS providers, multiple virtual machines share physical CPU cores. If neighboring '
                'instances consume excessive resources, your VPS experiences <strong>CPU Steal (%st)</strong>, where '
                'your virtual processor requests clock cycles from the hypervisor but is forced to wait.</p>\n'
                '  <p>Check <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">top</code> for the '
                '<code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">%st</code> value on the CPU line. '
                'If CPU steal exceeds 10% to 15% during crashes, contact your VPS hosting provider or migrate to a '
                'dedicated KVM instance with guaranteed compute resources.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">5. Cascading Failures in '
                'Apache and PHP-FPM</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Under traffic spikes, default Apache configurations spawn hundreds of concurrent worker '
                'processes. Each worker consumes 30MB to 70MB of RAM. When RAM is exhausted, the server starts swap '
                'thrashing, disk I/O skyrockets, and the server crashes in a cascading loop.</p>\n'
                '  <p>To permanently stabilize your web stack:</p>\n'
                '  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">\n'
                '    <li>Switch PHP-FPM pools from <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] '
                'text-navy">dynamic</code> to <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] '
                'text-navy">ondemand</code>.</li>\n'
                '    <li>Replace Apache with LiteSpeed Web Server to reduce RAM and CPU overhead by up to 70%.</li>\n'
                '    <li>Deploy a <a href="/cloudlinux-license" class="font-semibold text-brand '
                'hover:underline">CloudLinux OS license</a> on your <a href="/cpanel-license" class="font-semibold '
                'text-brand hover:underline">cPanel</a> or <a href="/plesk-license" class="font-semibold text-brand '
                'hover:underline">Plesk</a> servers to enforce hard memory and CPU limits per hosted tenant.</li>\n'
                '  </ul>\n'
                '</section>\n',
