                '<p class="text-lg text-gray-600">Stock Linux installations are engineered for broad hardware '
                'compatibility rather than high-performance server workloads. Whether you host high-traffic WordPress '
                'websites, SaaS APIs, or client hosting accounts, tuning your kernel parameters, networking stack, and '
                'runtime services can yield dramatic speed improvements. In this guide, we reveal 10 essential VPS '
                'optimization tricks to enhance throughput, reduce latency, and ensure rock-solid server '
                'stability.</p>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">1. Enabling Google TCP BBR '
                'Congestion Control</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Default Linux kernels use legacy Cubic congestion control, which throttles network throughput '
                "upon detecting minor packet loss. Google's <strong>TCP BBR</strong> measures bottleneck bandwidth and "
                'round-trip time directly, accelerating page delivery and asset downloads:</p>\n'
                '  <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-navy p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-100"><code># '
                'Add BBR congestion control to /etc/sysctl.conf\n'
                'net.core.default_qdisc = fq\n'
                'net.ipv4.tcp_congestion_control = bbr\n'
                '\n'
                '# Apply sysctl settings immediately\n'
                'sysctl -p\n'
                '\n'
                '# Verify BBR is active\n'
                'sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_congestion_control</code></pre>\n'
                '  <figure class="my-2"><img '
                'src="/assets/img/blog/10-vps-optimization-tricks-server-performance-stability-1.svg" alt="10 Linux '
                'VPS performance and stability optimization layers" width="960" height="420" loading="lazy" '
                'decoding="async" class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-gray-200" /><figcaption class="mt-2 '
                'text-center text-xs text-gray-500">Kernel network tuning, I/O scheduling, runtime optimization, and '
                'web server acceleration.</figcaption></figure>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">2. Tuning Virtual Memory and '
                'Swappiness (vm.swappiness = 10)</h2>\n'
                '  <p>The default Linux swappiness value of 60 causes the kernel to aggressively swap application '
                'memory to disk even when physical RAM is readily available. Reduce swappiness to keep active '
                'applications in high-speed RAM and configure dirty writeback ratios:</p>\n'
                '  <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-navy p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-100"><code># '
                'Add kernel VM parameters to /etc/sysctl.conf\n'
                'vm.swappiness = 10\n'
                'vm.vfs_cache_pressure = 50\n'
                'vm.dirty_ratio = 15\n'
                'vm.dirty_background_ratio = 5\n'
                '\n'
                '# Apply changes\n'
                'sysctl -p</code></pre>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">3. Increasing File Descriptors '
                'and Network Socket Backlogs</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Under heavy concurrent visitor traffic, Linux can run out of available file handles and socket '
                'backlog queues, dropping connections silently:</p>\n'
                '  <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-navy p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-100"><code># '
                'Add to /etc/sysctl.conf\n'
                'fs.file-max = 2097152\n'
                'net.core.somaxconn = 65535\n'
                'net.ipv4.tcp_max_syn_backlog = 8192\n'
                'net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range = 1024 65535\n'
                '\n'
                '# Increase limits in /etc/security/limits.conf\n'
                '* soft nofile 65535\n'
                '* hard nofile 65535</code></pre>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">4. Optimizing NVMe / SSD I/O '
                'Schedulers and TRIM</h2>\n'
                '  <p>On virtualized cloud storage and enterprise NVMe arrays, kernel I/O queuing introduces redundant '
                'CPU overhead. Set the I/O scheduler to <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] '
                'text-navy">none</code> or <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] '
                'text-navy">mq-deadline</code> and enable weekly filesystem TRIM timers:</p>\n'
                '  <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-navy p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-100"><code># '
                'Check current disk scheduler (example: vda)\n'
                'cat /sys/block/vda/queue/scheduler\n'
                '\n'
                '# Set to mq-deadline or none\n'
                'echo mq-deadline > /sys/block/vda/queue/scheduler\n'
                '\n'
                '# Enable automated weekly fstrim timer\n'
                'systemctl enable --now fstrim.timer</code></pre>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">5. Tuning PHP-FPM Process '
                'Pools and Memory Limits</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Misconfigured PHP-FPM pools cause either memory exhaustion or queued requests. Configure dynamic '
                'or ondemand process managers to prevent resource exhaustion:</p>\n'
                '  <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-navy p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-100"><code># '
                'Recommended pool configuration in /etc/php-fpm.d/www.conf\n'
                'pm = dynamic\n'
                'pm.max_children = 50\n'
                'pm.start_servers = 10\n'
                'pm.min_spare_servers = 5\n'
                'pm.max_spare_servers = 20\n'
                'pm.max_requests = 1000</code></pre>\n'
                '  <p>Setting <code class="rounded bg-mist px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-navy">pm.max_requests = '
                '1000</code> ensures worker processes periodically recycle, preventing long-term PHP memory leaks from '
                'degrading VPS stability.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">6. Accelerating Web Workloads '
                'with LiteSpeed Web Server</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Replacing standard Apache with an official <a href="/litespeed-license" class="font-semibold '
                'text-brand hover:underline">LiteSpeed license</a> is one of the highest-impact upgrades for any '
                "hosting VPS. LiteSpeed's asynchronous event architecture reduces CPU and memory usage by up to 70%, "
                'supports HTTP/3 and QUIC natively, and delivers server-level LSCache acceleration for WordPress, '
                'Magento, and XenForo.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">7. Database Performance Tuning '
                'with MariaDB InnoDB Buffers</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Default database configurations allocate meager memory buffers, forcing MySQL to read indexes '
                'from disk on every query. Allocate sufficient RAM to the InnoDB buffer pool:</p>\n'
                '  <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-navy p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-100"><code># '
                'Add to /etc/my.cnf.d/server.cnf or /etc/mysql/my.cnf\n'
                '[mysqld]\n'
                'innodb_buffer_pool_size = 1G\n'
