                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4 tracking-tight">In-Depth Architectural '
                'Analysis: Why Apache Stalls Under Traffic Spikes</h2>\n'
                '  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">To truly understand why LiteSpeed outperforms Apache on a '
                "VPS, we must examine what happens inside the Linux kernel during high concurrency. Apache's standard "
                'Multi-Processing Modules (MPMs) rely on either preforking child processes or managing worker thread '
                'pools. Each incoming HTTP request is assigned to a thread or process that remains occupied for the '
                'entire duration of the transaction—including slow mobile downloads and keep-alive delays.</p>\n'
                '  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">When hundreds of users browse simultaneously, the kernel '
                'incurs massive CPU context-switching overhead as it swaps between hundreds of active processes. '
                'Furthermore, each Apache worker process consumes between 20MB and 60MB of RAM. On a 4GB VPS, running '
                'out of physical RAM forces the operating system to write pages to disk swap, causing disk I/O '
                'saturation and severe latency spikes (5,000ms+ response times).</p>\n'
                '  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">LiteSpeed, in contrast, uses a single-threaded '
                'asynchronous event loop (or a small fixed pool of worker loops matching your licensed worker count). '
                'Network sockets are registered with the Linux <code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-sm '
                'text-gray-800">epoll</code> subsystem. LiteSpeed only executes code when data is actually ready to be '
                'read or written. This eliminates idle connection memory overhead and enables sub-50ms response times '
                'even when handling 10,000+ open sockets.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4 tracking-tight">WordPress Optimization: '
                'Apache + W3 Total Cache vs LiteSpeed + LSCache</h2>\n'
                '  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Many WordPress site owners attempt to match LiteSpeed '
                'speed by stacking WordPress caching plugins (W3 Total Cache, WP Super Cache, or WP Rocket) on Apache. '
                'However, PHP-based caching plugins still require invoking the PHP runtime engine and executing '
                'thousands of lines of PHP code just to serve a cached HTML page from disk.</p>\n'
                '  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">LiteSpeed Cache (LSCache) is implemented entirely in '
                'compiled C/C++ inside the web server kernel. When a visitor requests a cached page, LiteSpeed serves '
