                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">3. Real-World Case Study: Resolving Chronic '
                'Shared Hosting Infections</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Consider a typical scenario faced by shared hosting providers: a cPanel VPS hosting 140 client '
                'websites begins experiencing recurring malware spikes. The symptoms are familiar:</p>\n'
                '  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">\n'
                '    <li><strong>Symptom 1:</strong> Several client WordPress sites redirect visitors to spam websites '
                'when opened from mobile browsers or search engine links.</li>\n'
                "    <li><strong>Symptom 2:</strong> The hosting provider\\'s support team runs manual Maldet or "
                'ClamAV scans, identifying 40 infected PHP files. When these files are deleted, the affected WordPress '
                'sites crash with fatal 500 errors.</li>\n'
                "    <li><strong>Symptom 3:</strong> Within 48 hours, the infection reappears because the attacker\\'s "
                'backdoor web shell was heavily obfuscated with <code>eval(base64_decode())</code> and bypassed static '
