                '    <p>exiqgrep -f "spammer@compromiseddomain.com" -i | xargs exim -Mrm</p>\n'
                '  </div>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Step 5: Repairing Corrupted Exim Retry '
                'Databases</h2>\n'
                '  <p>If Exim refuses to retry messages or continues deferring domains despite successful manual SMTP '
                'connections, the local Berkeley DB files in <code>/var/spool/exim/db/</code> may be corrupted. These '
                'databases record retry timestamps, rate limits, and host callout caches.</p>\n'
                '  <p>Clean and rebuild the retry databases safely from the terminal:</p>\n'
                '  <div class="bg-gray-900 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-xs text-gray-100 overflow-x-auto">\n'
                '    <p class="text-gray-400"># Purge expired retry records</p>\n'
                '    <p>/usr/sbin/exim_tidydb -t 1d /var/spool/exim retry</p>\n'
                '    <p class="text-gray-400 mt-2"># If the database is completely locked or corrupted, stop Exim and '
                'clear hints:</p>\n'
                '    <p>/scripts/restartsrv_exim --stop</p>\n'
                '    <p>rm -f /var/spool/exim/db/retry*</p>\n'
                '    <p>rm -f /var/spool/exim/db/wait*</p>\n'
                '    <p>/scripts/restartsrv_exim --start</p>\n'
                '  </div>\n'
                '  <p>Exim will recreate fresh database files automatically upon startup, clearing all false retry '
                'locks.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Proactive Outbound Protection with '
