                '<p class="text-lg text-gray-600">On a shared hosting server hosting dozens or hundreds of accounts, '
                'network bandwidth is a shared commodity. When overall server bandwidth suddenly spikes—threatening to '
                "exceed monthly data transfer allocations or saturate the server's network uplink—identifying which "
                'specific cPanel user is responsible is the first order of business. Whether the spike is caused by a '
                "customer's viral video, a misconfigured backup job, image hotlinking, or an unindexed web scraper, "
                'cPanel &amp; WHM provides robust reporting tools to isolate the culprit. This guide walks through '
                'both WHM web-based dashboards and command-line forensics to find and manage high-bandwidth accounts '
                'on your <a href="/cpanel-license" class="text-blue-600 font-semibold hover:underline">cPanel '
                'server</a>.</p>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bandwidth Tracking Mechanics in cPanel &amp; '
                'WHM</h2>\n'
                '  <p>cPanel tracks data transfer using its internal statistics daemon, <code>cpanellogd</code>:</p>\n'
                '  <figure class="my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 bg-gray-900/5 p-2">\n'
                '  <img src="/assets/img/blog/find-cpanel-account-using-most-bandwidth-guide-1.svg" alt="cPanel and '
                'WHM bandwidth consumption analysis and triage workflow diagram" width="800" height="420" '
                'class="w-full h-auto rounded-lg shadow-sm" loading="lazy">\n'
                '  <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 text-center mt-2 px-2">Figure 1: Investigating bandwidth '
                'consumers across WHM GUI, CLI API queries, and web domlogs.</figcaption>\n'
                '</figure>\n'
                '  <p>The daemon processes access logs across four independent subsystems:</p>\n'
                '  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700">\n'
                '    <li><strong>HTTP/HTTPS Web Traffic:</strong> Extracted from Apache/LiteSpeed domain transfer logs '
                'in <code>/var/log/apache2/domlogs/</code>.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>Email Traffic:</strong> Extracted from Exim and Dovecot logs (SMTP outbound and '
                'IMAP/POP3 downloads).</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>FTP Transfers:</strong> Extracted from FTP transfer logs.</li>\n'
                '  </ul>\n'
                '  <p>Data is compiled into RRD databases stored in <code>/var/cpanel/bandwidth/</code>, making it '
                'accessible through both graphical reports and API commands.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Method 1: WHM Graphical Bandwidth '
                'Reporter</h2>\n'
                '  <p>If you have access to the WHM web interface, you can sort all accounts by data transfer in '
                'seconds:</p>\n'
                '  <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700">\n'
                '    <li>Log into WHM as <code>root</code>.</li>\n'
                '    <li>Navigate to <em>Home &raquo; Account Information &raquo; View Bandwidth Usage</em>.</li>\n'
                '    <li>The dashboard displays a table containing every hosted account. Click on the <strong>Usage '
                '(Megabytes)</strong> column header to sort descending.</li>\n'
                '    <li>Click on any specific account name to view an in-depth breakdown categorized by protocol '
                '(HTTP, IMAP, SMTP, FTP) and day of the month.</li>\n'
                '  </ol>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Method 2: Command-Line WHM API '
                'Forensics</h2>\n'
                '  <p>When working via SSH terminal, query the WHM API directly to rank users by total consumed '
                'bytes:</p>\n'
                '  <div class="bg-gray-900 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-xs text-gray-100 overflow-x-auto">\n'
                '    <p class="text-gray-400"># Query WHM API for bandwidth stats and sort by total gigabytes</p>\n'
                '    <p>whmapi1 showbw | awk \'/user:/{u=$2} /totalbytes:/{print $2/1024/1024/1024 " GB - " u}\' | '
                'sort -n -r | head -n 10</p>\n'
                '  </div>\n'
                '  <p>If a single user shows 500 GB while others average 2 GB, you have immediately isolated the '
                'offending account.</p>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Method 3: Parsing Apache Domlogs for '
                'Real-Time Transfers</h2>\n'
                "  <p>If the spike is actively happening right now and cPanel's nightly <code>cpanellogd</code> cycle "
                'has not yet processed the numbers, check the live size of the Apache domain transfer logs:</p>\n'
                '  <div class="bg-gray-900 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-xs text-gray-100 overflow-x-auto">\n'
                '    <p class="text-gray-400"># Find the largest active domain log file today</p>\n'
                '    <p>ls -lhS /var/log/apache2/domlogs/ | head -n 15</p>\n'
                '    <p class="text-gray-400 mt-2"># Sum bandwidth transferred in a specific domain log file (in '
                'MB)</p>\n'
                '    <p>awk \'{sum += $10} END {print sum / 1024 / 1024 " MB"}\' '
                '/var/log/apache2/domlogs/example.com</p>\n'
                '  </div>\n'
                '  <p>To identify the exact URL being downloaded repeatedly:</p>\n'
                '  <div class="bg-gray-900 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-xs text-gray-100 overflow-x-auto">\n'
                '    <p class="text-gray-400"># Display top 10 most requested URLs in the domain log</p>\n'
                "    <p>awk '{print $7}' /var/log/apache2/domlogs/example.com | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n "
                '10</p>\n'
                '  </div>\n'
                '</section>\n'
                '\n'
                '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
                '  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Preventative Mitigations</h2>\n'
                '  <p>Once you identify the cause, implement protective guardrails:</p>\n'
                '  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700">\n'
                '    <li><strong>Enforce Bandwidth Limits in Packages:</strong> In WHM <em>Edit a Package</em>, set '
                'realistic bandwidth limits (e.g., 50 GB/month) with automated suspension when exceeded.</li>\n'
                '    <li><strong>Enable Cloudflare Proxy:</strong> Offload static assets (images, video, PDFs) to '
                "Cloudflare's global edge network, reducing direct server bandwidth by up to 80%.</li>\n"
                '    <li><strong>Deploy CloudLinux Network Limits:</strong> An enterprise <a '
                'href="/cloudlinux-license" class="text-blue-600 font-semibold hover:underline">CloudLinux OS '
                'license</a> allows administrators to enforce per-user network throughput caps (e.g., maximum 50 '
                "Mbit/s per account), preventing any single tenant from saturating the server's network card.</li>\n"
                '  </ul>\n'
                '</section>\n',
